-
-			  Load Spikes on Ultrix
-				traced to
-			 Bogus Select Collisions
-
-		 Corey Satten, corey@cac.washington.edu
-		   Networks and Distributed Computing
-			University of Washington
-			   Seattle, Washington
-				 9/24/91
-
-If you have been experiencing load spikes on your Ultrix system, the
-following may be of considerable interest to you.
-
-Background
-
-    The implementation of the select(2) system call found in versions of
-    BSD up though 4.3 Tahoe (not to imply anything about later versions)
-    has a simple but expensive way of handling "select collisions".  True
-    "collisions" occur very rarely and refer to a situation in which two
-    or more processes are blocked in select on a single device or network
-    connection.  Since the data structures used to wake up processes when
-    they have input contain space for only a single process id, when two
-    or more processes must be awakened, all processes blocked in select
-    for any reason are slightly awakened and most quickly roll over and
-    go back to sleep.  If any of these processes are swapped out, some
-    small part of them is swapped back in before they go back to sleep.
-
-    All X clients, when idle, are blocked in select, so if you have
-    a lot of X clients and you experience a select collision, a large
-    number of processes can be affected.  If these programs happen to run
-    at the very moment the load average measurements are done, a very
-    large load spike can result.
-
-    A short program, spike.c, (enclosed) will generate a true select
-    collisions for each newline typed.
-
-Ultrix Bugs
-
-    RISC Ultrix version 4.1 and 4.2 (not to imply anything about other
-    versions) generates select collisions in circumstances which do
-    not appear to be proper.  I call these "bogus select collisions".
-    On our system the collisions are quite common (often several per
-    minute) and affect roughly 300 processes out of 550 on our system.
-
-    Two short programs: spike2.c and spike3.c (enclosed) will generate
-    bogus select collisions only on our Ultrix systems.
-
-    It is unclear whether select collisions are mostly a measurement
-    anomaly or a real performance problem.  Clearly there is some
-    unnecessary disk I/O associated with them and some CPU time is wasted
-    but they are over almost as soon as they start so it's hard to tell
-    how much of a problem they really are.  They certainly make the load
-    average a useless number on our system since it spikes from a baseline
-    of around one up to as much as 40 and then exponentially decays back
-    down (often only part way before the next spike).
-
-Detecting the problem
-
-    There are several ways to look for select collisions on Ultrix.  The
-    most direct is to examine the kernel variable which counts them.  It
-    is called "nselcoll" and can be viewed with my kmem program (previously
-    posted and included below for completeness) or with dbx:
-
-	kmem nselcoll
-	echo print nselcoll | dbx -k /vmunix
-
-    You can also see the effect of the select collisions by running:
-
-	vmstat -v 5
-
-    and watching the number of "sleepers" (sl:  the number of processes
-    which have not run within the last 20 seconds).  When a select collision
-    happens, the number will jump to include all the processes blocked in
-    select:
-
-	% vmstat -v 5
-	      procs            memory
-	  r dw pw sl  w    vm  avm   rm  arm  fre
-	  1  0  0 86  0  203k  69k  93k  37k 4564
-	  0  0  0 80  0  202k  62k  92k  35k 4612
-	  1  0  0 80  0  202k  62k  92k  32k 4740
-	  1  0  0294  0  202k 153k  92k  78k 4516
-	  0  0  0288  0  202k 153k  92k  77k 4404
-	  0  0  0287  0  202k 151k  92k  76k 4364
-	  1  0  0286  0  202k 151k  92k  76k 4344
-	  0  0  0 65  0  202k  53k  92k  28k 4344
-	  3  0  0 66  0  202k  58k  92k  31k 4344
-
-    The more "sleepers" were swapped out, the longer it takes the system
-    to wake them and then put them back to sleep so the greater the chance
-    of having the collision show up as a spike in the load average.
-
-Programs which generate "bogus select collisions"
-
-    X clients which use XtAddInput (to incorporate a pipe into the list
-    of file descriptors watched in the X event loop) or in some other way
-    cause the X event loop to select on a pipe seem to be the largest
-    source of bogus select collisions.  Xmh is probably a widely available
-    example of this but the work I've done has been on home-grown clients
-    so there is a chance xmh is doing something different.
-
-    Without getting into any custom software, you can generate collisions
-    on the console by starting and ending a dxterm from the session
-    manager (dxsession).
-
-    As an aid to tracking down this problem, I hacked our kernel to save
-    the PID of the process which generates the collision.  As a result,
-    I have a list of processes which have "collided", (though I don't have
-    what they collided with).  I find the *vast* majority are caused by
-    IslandLIC (3rd party software) and xmh (the X version of the mh mail
-    agent) however the following other programs have also shown up:
-
-	Xprompter		dxcardfiler
-	emacs			rlogin
-	rlogind			rsh
-	rshd			sh
-	telnet			telnetd
-	xterm
-
-Closing remarks
-
-    I haven't tried to fix the kernel and I'm not sure I will.  I'm curious
-    what feedback I'll get from posting what I've discovered so far.
-    I assume it's too much to hope that someone has already found and fixed
-    this but asking is cheap...
-
