-/*
- * This program demonstrates a quirk in the implementation of
- * select on 4.3BSD, Ultrix 4.1, SUNOS 4.1.1 (and maybe others).
- *
- * Looking at the kernel source for the routine "selwakeup", I find
- * that if there is more than one process using select to wait for
- * I/O on the same device; when I/O is ready, *ALL* processes on the
- * system sleeping on "selwait" (waiting for any select) are awakened.
- * Since most have no input they don't seem to run much (judging from
- * how many can "run" like this per second and have no significant
- * impact on idle CPU).  If, however, any were swapped out, they swap
- * in (or maybe only a tiny part swaps in since so little disk
- * traffic results from the swapping) and this stretches the real-time
- * over which the number of processes "runable" is high and increases
- * the chances that a system load average spike will result.  I believe
- * the load spike is mostly a measurement anomaly however the active
- * real memory does goes up a little.  Another indication of these
- * processes running is that the "SL" field of a "ps avx" shows all
- * of these processes have recently run.  On ultrix, the "vmstat -v 5"
- * command will show the number of "sl" (short term sleepers) has also
- * jumped accordingly.
- *
- * To see which processes are waiting in select, do a "ps axl" and look
- * at the WCHAN field.  If it is numeric, do a "nm /vmunix|grep selwait"
- * and look for the least significant part of that number in the WCHAN
- * field; otherwise, look for the word "selwait" directly in the ps list.
- * All the processes in selwait will be affected -- on our system, of
- * the roughly 550 processes, there are about 300 in selwait.
- *
- * To generate a spike, run this program and type return for each spike
- *
- * Corey Satten, corey@cac.washington.edu 5/24/91
- */
-#include <stdio.h>
-#include <fcntl.h>
-main(){
-    int i;
-    char buf[100];
-    if (fcntl(0,F_SETFL,O_NDELAY) < 0) {
-	fprintf(stderr, "fcntl failed\n");
-	exit(1);
