	In terms of the "war stories" on VAXen being able to survive, I
	can remember two of them:

	* A VSII GPX being returned to the Mill in Maynard by a vendor who
	  had completed his software port and the trucker dropped it off
	  of the loading dock (about 4'). The fall bent one of the casters
	  completely back under the card cage (not simple to do since the
	  BA123 "world box" was a pretty rugged cookie). But, when we got
	  it into the lab it booted without a hitch (and we didn't even
	  open it to reseat the Q-bus cards).

	* A service call that was placed to DEC on 17.OCT.89 (the last big
	  quake here on the left coast) by a company in San Francisco. It
	  seems that their VAX 11/785 dropped through the floor during the
	  quake. From the description I heard, the cabinet door was popped
	  open and some of the cards had been knocked loose. The customer
	  said that he pushed the cards back in, forced the door closed,
	  plugged an extension cord into on its new floor and rebooted. Now
	  that's a tough machine.


	Ahh, memories...


				Thanx,
				  AJ


