	Folks,

	  I've been following this discussion for a while now and I want
	to make a couple of points and repeat my previous offer.


	1. I take no issue with any customer's decision to buy Third Party
	   Hardware. I don't now, nor have I ever, believed that any system
	   vendor should attempt to dictate a customer's environment or
	   purchasing practices. I, and an increasing number of my DEC
	   colleagues, believe that DEC needs to work with Third Party vendors
	   to do the best integration job that we can. The requirements
	   for such integration require a great deal of time and effort.
	   And in many cases, we have no reasonable way to regression test
	   against all of the possible configurations in the market.

	2. At some point, somebody is going to say; "well, just make it a
	   standard SCSI interface and it'll be fine". Well, DEC does use
	   a standard SCSI interface. The NCR53C94 controller chip in the
	   DS5000/xxx series machines supports a full SCSI 2 implementation
	   (so, for that matter, does the SII chip in the DS2100/DS3100).
	   And, with the Ultrix v4.2 release, the Ultrix SCSI driver is
	   completely SCSI 2 compliant.

	   Additionally, as I have mentioned in several previous postings,
	   DEC is re-writing the Ultrix SCSI driver to a CAM (Common Access
	   Method) interface. This is a proposed IEEE standard from an
	   industry committee to make the software interface for SCSI an
	   equivalent to the ANSI SCSI specification (which defines a
	   hardware protocol only).

	   While I am not certain where the issue lies with the Fujitsu disk
	   drive, not all instances of "it worked before and now it doesn't"
	   are DEC's fault.

		Example:

			I contacted a customer who had posted here with a
			complaint that a Third Party Vendor's disk drive
			(which had functioned fine on a DS3100 and a SPARC 1)
			failed to operate properly on a DS5000/200. The
			customer wanted to know what DEC had "broken". The
			disk drive was promoted as a SCSI 2 implementation.

			We got the disk drive vendor to send us a sample disk
			and put it up on a SCSI analyzer. Here's the story:

			* The DS3100 (and apparently the SPARCstation 1)
			  do not issue a SCSI bus reset to clear the devices
			  on the SCSI bus to a known state.

			* The ANSI SCSI 1 specification did not provide a
			  recommended time for response to a SCSI bus reset
			  (that is, the timeframe in which the device is to
			  respond to the SCSI host with it's SCSI ID#).

			* The ANSI SCSI 2 specification recommends a maximum
			  response time of 250ms to a SCSI bus reset.

			* The DS5000/200 system SCSI controller will wait for
			  240ms, reset the timer and wait for a further 240ms
			  prior to ignoring the unresponsive SCSI device.

			* The Third Party disk drive was not responding to a
			  SCSI bus reset within 635ms.

			* We helped the disk vendor to debug their firmware
			  to a more SCSI 2 compliant source.

			
			In the final analysis, who was wrong? The disk vendor
			for not following the recommended response time? Or,
			Digital, for using a standard SCSI command? It DOESN'T
			matter. What DOES matter is the customer's need for
			the disk drive to run on DEC hardware. And, with the
			co-operation of the disk vendor, that's what they now
			have.

			Additionally, we have generated a new SCSI PROM for
			the DS5000/200 and the PMAZ-AA SCSI host adapter. It
			is much more forgiving of Third Party Hardware. You
			can find information about this on:

			gatekeeper	16.1.0.2	pub/DEC/TriAdd


