In article <1991Sep23.200807.16699@mnemosyne.cs.du.edu>, aduell@isis.cs.du.edu (Tony Duell) writes:
|>    Owing to DEC's rediculous licence charges (they are certainly
|> rediculous if your are a hobbyist!!), a few friends and myself are
|> thinking of writting an OS for PDP11's. As I already know a bit about OS-9
|> (I used to run it on my Tandy CoCO 3, and still do sometimes!), I am
|> wondering just how much of the concepts of the I/O system are copyrighted
|> to Microware, and how much I could use in my OS without finding myself in
|> court/ Specifically, I would like to know if I could use the following
|> features:
|> 
|> 1) The idea of separate device drivers and descriptors. It would make life
|> a lot easier if this were allowed.
|> 2) The existance of the device address in the descriptor
|> 3) The fact that disk sectors are simply numbered from 0 to <max # of
|> sectors >-1. Again, I like this idea, and wonder if I could use it. It
|> makes a lot more sense than track/sector numbers
|> 4) Named memory modules
|> 
|> That's enough to start with. Please reply to the e-mail address given
|> below, or post to the net.
|> 

DISCLAIMERS:
- I have no particular expertise in the area of copyright as it applies to
  operating systems.
- any statements I make herein are my personal views and not those of my 
  employer.

In general, my understanding of copyright is that it covers the specific
expression of ideas but not the concepts behind those ideas.  Based on that,
I'd think there would be little grounds for action if the general concepts
were adopted in a new system that was redesigned as well as being translated 
into a different implementation language.  Unless the system was originally
designed for a similar (or maybe identical) instruction set and I/O architecture
it seems a redesign would entail sufficient change to avoid the issue of 
copyright infringement.  You might still need to look at the licence details
to make sure you don't risk violating them though.

I am not particularly familar with OS-9 so I can't specifically discuss that.
When was OS-9 first written?  For what architecture?  What other architectures
has it been ported to?

It seems to me that some of the specific points mentioned are terminology as
much as anything else.  The separation of I/O device driver code and the
databases describing the device hardware is a well-established concept in
Digital's RSX-11 family of operating systems (developed circa 1974).  Device 
addresses as a part of the device driver database were a refinement of the 
basic concept as it evolved over the years.  Device hardware addresses are
very much dependent upon the particular hardware I/O architecture, with major
differences between "port numbers" and memory-mapped controller register addresses.  I would think this feature would be too general a concept to 
protect by copyright.

The numbering of disk blocks sequentially beginning with zero is another concept
that I believe has been well-established for years.  I personally have been
familiar with it since 1978, and have good reason to believe it dates from much
before then.  In Digital's RSX-11 and VMS operating systems the terminology for
this scheme is "logical block numbering".  I have no idea what it might be named
in other systems.

Hope this helps.  I'm afraid I can't really offer much more help, because
developing PDP-11 operating systems is part of my job...

--bruce mcculley


