From recient history at a large southern university:

The moving of a VAX 11/750 CPU cabinet was undertaken.  The 'source' building
(if you will) had a loading dock but no lift.  In order to get the 11/750 down
into the 3/4 pickup truck (which you can call the 'medium of transport') a heavy
duty hand dolly was used.  *Thump* into that back of the truck. 

At the 'destination' building there was no loading dock.  So an idea was brought
forth: lower it out of the back of the pickup like the it was lowered into it.
Ok, sounds like a decient plan.

At this point let us step back from our heros and examine this problem.  What
does this piece of equipment weigh?  In excess of 500 pounds.  How far does it
need to be lowered?  About 3 1/2 feet.  Using what?  A hand dolly.  And how many
heros are attempting this feat?  Three. 

Back to our soon to be engineers, we find two holding the hand dolly at the top 
and one guiding/supporting/holding 500 pounds at the bottom.  The 11/750 is 
lowered 1 foot... 2 feet... 3 feet, and dropped 6 inches. *Bounce* (yes it did 
bounce.  Those puppies make a nice sound when they do that, too).  This action 
caused the guy at the bottom to fall backwards and land firmly on his butt
(and it hurt).

Well, the 11/750 was rolled up to the 3rd floor, plugged in and turned on.

Booted the first time, didn't have any damage and has run successfully for over 
a year and a half with no maintenance.  Sturdy little buggers, arn't they?

