>In article <1991Sep17.043506.16965@uokmax.ecn.uoknor.edu>,
>sspatter@uokmax.ecn.uoknor.edu (Samuel S Patterson) writes:
>|>      Okay, rather simple question.  When I hit control halt/sys on
>|>my PDP The SR DISP light goes out, the buss error light comes on, and
>|>the address data panel stays on.  I did the system tests the book sug-
>|>gests.  IE, pulling out the M9302 card at the end of the unibus. Anyways,
>|>The results of the tests tell me that I have a BUS Grant problem. What
>|>I need to know is how to fix that.  Where is the bus grant generated and

Yes, you have a buss grant problem.  The simplest method to eliminte potential
problems is using the following procedure:

1. Remove the buss jumper between the CPU backplane and the rest of the
   system - this effectively isolates the CPU and whatever else is in that
   backplane and removes LOTS of potential sources of confusion.
2. Ensure that there is a grant card in each slot, AND that they are
   facing the correct direction.  (without looking at one, I can't offhand
   tell you which direction that is...)
3. Turn on the computer - you should now be able to successfully halt the
   processor.  If not, the number of potential problems has been greatly
   reduced.  If you were able to halt the computer, continue with the next
   steps after reconnecting the next backplane and performing step 2 again.
4. Check the power supply!  This is a common cause of system hangups.
5. Look at the rear of the backplane and check the NPR jumper pins - you'll
   have to look in the book but I believe they are pins A1/B1 on the C
   section of the backplane connector.  They should be jumpered from one
   slot to the next.
6. If all the above fail, remove everything from the backplane except for
   the processor boards and the console interface board and put grant cards
   in the empty slots.  If the machine still doesn't work you either have a
   bad processor board or a bad console interface board.

It would greatly help to know exactly what configuration the machine is in,
this would make evaluating the problem a bit easier.

By the way, I have daily (or at least frequent) contact with a large number
of 11/34a and 11/44 processors, so...

You can e-mail me directly for more information.

