In article <1991Sep24.151341.13776@colorado.edu> grunwald@foobar.colorado.edu (Dirk Grunwald) writes:


>>   Given that the DS5000/120's can do SCSI-2, is there a reason to use
>>   this over SCSI-1? We have a FUJI 2266S that is configured for SCSI-1
>>   -- would using the SCSI-2 protocol speed anything up?



	Now that's a challenge. Can I answer this without writing a reply
	of "War and Peace" length? ;-)

	Well, here's my understanding (obviously open to interpretation):

	SCSI-1	ANSI X3.131-1986

	  Was an ANSI specification based on the SASI protocol (Shugart
	Associates Smart Interface...as in Alan Shugart who founded
	SEAGATE). It basically addressed winchester disks and floppies.

	
	SCSI-1 CCS

	  Was a version in which people used the Common Command Set (CCS
	was a working paper which attempted to clarify the SCSI-1 commands)
	and provided some rudimentary tape drive interfaces as well. It 
	should be noted that while the CCS document was ratified by a small
	group of SCSI vendors (both system and option) and is used by a
	number of SCSI-1 devices and systems. Additionally, it became the
	basis for the SCSI-2 specification.



	SCSI-2 	ANSI X3.131-199x, rev 10d

	  Is expected to be approved before the end of the calendar year
	with no significant change to the technical information. SCSI-2
	continues on with the CCS work and defines models for several
	classes of devices. Instruction sets were included to support new
	device classes like: CD-ROM, WORM, MO, printers and scanners.


	CAM

	  The Common Access Method is an industry committee definition that
	is being proposed to the IEEE as the SCSI software standard interface.
	It is intended to do for SCSI software interfaces the same thing that
	the ANSI SCSI specifications did for the wire protocol.



	SCSI-3

	  The ANSI committee is sitting now to formulate the SCSI-3 protocol
	which will likely bring you features such as: enhancements of
	multiple host performance and more addressable devices on the bus.




	Now that we have a common glossary, why would you want to use SCSI-2?

	* You get better feature/command support for a variety of SCSI devices
	  than you did with SCSI-1.

	* Support for Third Party Hardware is considerably easier.

	* With the CAM implementation layered above SCSI-2 you can get access
	  to support for disks, tapes, optical memory, CD-ROMs, scanners,
	  printers and communication.


	If I buy a host system that implements SCSI-2, will SCSI-1 devices
	work with it?

	That is the goal. If the SCSI-1 implementation is clean to the spec
	and pays attention to the CCS working paper, your chances are very
	good. If the implementation is not clean, such a device can cause
	frequent hangs of the SCSI bus.

	
	If I have a SCSI-1 system/host, can I use SCSI-2 devices with it?

	That is a more difficult situation. One example of an item which
	might go awry is that SCSI-2 allows a device to negotiate for the
	use of synchronous transfer whether or not the host has inquired
	for it. In some SCSI-1 hosts this will cause the bus to hang. There
	are no guarantees of compatibility between a SCSI-1 host and SCSI-2
	devices.


	I thought that SCSI-2 was guaranteed to run 10MB/s?

	The SCSI-2 standard makes allowances for a variety of implementations.
	Basically, there are two types of cabling for SCSI buses to use. The
	most common in the market is single-ended while the next generation
	is likely to be differential.


	Bus implementation		clock rate		cable length
	==================		==========		============

	Single-ended			   5MHz			    6M

	Differential			   5MHz			   25M

	Fast Single-ended		  10MHz			    18" ~0.5M

	Fast Differential		  10MHz			   25M



	Data Rates by implementation


	Bus Implementation	BYTE		WORD 		LONGWORD
	==================      ====            ====            ========

	SCSI-2			5MB/s		10MB/s		  20MB/s

	Fast SCSI-2		10MB/s		20MB/s		  40MB/s


		NOTE: The standard SCSI-2 interface is an 8bit data path.
		      Wide SCSI-2 normally refers to a 16bit (word) data path
		      and Very Wide SCSI-2 refers to a 32bit (longword) data
		      path. You should also be aware that while you can put
		      an 8bit device on a 16 or 32bit bus, you can not mix
		      single-ended and differential systems. You should also
		      be aware of the connector changes as you increase your
		      data path.

	So, while you can get some very high (relatively) data rates out of
	the SCSI-2 specification, the standard transfer rate is still 5MB/s.
	Many of the device vendors are waiting for system vendors to have an
	implementation of a faster host adapter before they begin to provide
	SCSI devices that run the same data rates (it's a chicken and egg
	decision). The only large system vendor that I know of currently
	running a Fast SCSI-2 implementation is HP who has an EISA version
	for Fast Differential SCSI-2 (they offer one disk drive in a 1.3GB
	capacity as their only Fast Differential SCSI-2 model). But, I do
	expect to see other system vendors moving to the higher data rates.


	It looks like my reply did turn into "War and Peace", but I hope that
	you find the information helpful.

				Thanx,
				  AJ


