I can't help you with RT-11, as I haven't even seen it since I was in
college. However, I have worked with Whitesmiths C from that era, and
I'll pass on what lore I can recall.

Whitesmiths C differs from K&R C in two fundamental ways. The first is
related to external variable definitions. The basic rule is to
completely ignore the presence or absence of the "extern" keyword. If a
variable which is described outside of a function is *initialized*, then
the description is a definition, otherwise it is a declaration. Thus the
following are all definitions:

	int x = 0;
	extern int y = 0;
	extern union {
		int	i;
		float	f;
	} u = 0;

While all of the following are declarations:

	int x;
	extern int y;
	extern union {
		int	i;
		float	f;
	};


The second major difference is in the function libraries. The rules here
are, "If it has the same name as a K&R function, it does something
different," and, "If it does the same function as a K&R function, it has
a different name." For example, "printf" is called "putfmt", and the
format characters are different (like "%i" instead of "%d"). More good
examples are the string functions: "strlen" is "lenstr", while "strcpy"
is called "cpystr".

Have fun -- the company I was working for got tired of retraining
programmers, so we rewrote both the compiler and the run-time library
(we had sources). You might be better off with the DECUS PDP-11 C
compiler, if it still exists.

--
