>
>	In terms of the "war stories" on VAXen being able to survive

This isn't about a whole VAX, but the SBI backplane:

Back in early 1987, the group I worked for sold their VAX8600 (at a very
good price) and replaced it with a donated 11/780 and a bunch of uVAXes in
an SILINK cluster.  We kept some of the cards from the 8600, such as the 
second UNIBUS adapter, to use in the 11/780. What I didn't know was that the 
8600 had a complete SBI backplane for the cards to plug into, whereas the 780 
needed individual pieces of it to connect together. So there we were with 
adapter boards and nothing to plug them into.

Then the DEC field service guy said "You know, I think I saw something that
looked like what we need, sitting under the table next door" in the room
with printers and tape drives. Sure enough, there it was; it had been there,
gathering dust, getting kicked around, for the two years since the installation
of the 8600 (which itself replaced an older 780). He vacuumed it, installed
it, and it worked like a charm. Last time I checked a few months ago (I don't
work there any more), it was still working. Tough piece of equipment.

Incidentally, the 8600 was moved about a mile away into a basement office in
an old house. I heard that the movers dropped it down the stairs - but it
worked fine anyway. 
----------
On another topic, my first boss used to say that boards made using TTL were so 
tough you could stomp on them and they'd still work. :-) I know I've 
occasionally pulled ancient UNIBUS boards out of storage - just piled up in 
a cardboard box - and they work just fine. The hard part is figuring out
which ones are which in the jumble.
