In article <1991Sep27.154023.3095@verdix.com>, esther@verdix.com (Esther Lumsdon) writes:
> If you were me, would you purchase a genuine DEC disk, or replace the existing
> 100MB teensy system disk with a 3rd party SCSI drive that's already sitting 
> around?

The "liberal" side of the argument is that DEC has claimed that they're 
committed to supporting the SCSI standard, and that at least some people in DEC
know full well that they can't compete in the SCSI disk "commodity" market,
nor can they compete in the workstation market if their strategy includes
forcing people to buy their disk drives.  

The "conservative" side is that (DEC claims) that the SCSI standard is 
insufficiently specified to guarantee that drive X will always work with
controller Y, for all cases of X and Y.  

There are some SCSI disks that just won't work on VAXstations.  There is one
CD-ROM drive, a Hitachi model, that worked for a while but won't under 5.3, 
due to changes in timing constants in the SCSI driver.  

I have a client with a 1.2G Wren VII on a VS3100 and it works just fine. 

My opinion is that there is insufficient data to give a blanket "yes" or "no".
So what would I recommend?  Well, if the VS3100 in question is one that you
absolutely *must* have *maximum possible reliability* on, get a bigger DEC
drive.  If you can afford to fiddle with things on occasion to make them work,
go 3rd-party.  

I don't remember the track record of the drives yo mentioned on 3100's.  If I
were you I would try to trade your drive for a Wren of equivalent capacity --
the Wrens seem always to have worked on the 3100's.  
 
