In article <1991Sep9.192716.14199@decus.com.au> geremin@decus.com.au writes:
>	Well, lets start at the beginning - the VAX 11/780 was 
>based on the PDP-11 instruction set with 32-bit data.

No, the VAX is a completely different processor than the PDP.  The
architecture is *not* similar. The PDP uses word opcodes that
include addressing modes. The VAX uses byte opcodes with trailing
byte sequences for arguments. Some VAXes (11/78x, 88xx) have PDP
*emulation* modes.


>                                                      It used a 
>PDP-11/23 buried down in the bottom to get it going.

No, the console PDP of an 11/78x series VAX is T-11 based.


>						     Some say
>that the PDP-11 was necessary to load MICROcode every time the VAX
>was restarted

Some people are wrong, then. The PDP loads microcode into the VAX,
but you only have to do this once per machine reset. You can
halt/start the VAX to your heart's content without reloading the
microcode.


>		   - some (PDP-11 SIG types) say that the upper
>deck processing was all `show and tell' and that all the real
>crunching was done by the 11/23 chip.

This is noise.


>				      This, of course, explains
>why the VAX-11/782 (or was it the VAX-11/785) had to have the
>floating point accelerator chip added to the PDP-11/23 board to
>achieve the enhanced performance on the later models.

The above noise does not explain anything.

The 11/782 is nothing more than two 11/780s joined with a
"multi-port memory" (shared processor memory) cabinet.  The 11/785
is a later/faster implementation of the 11/780. Further, the 11/785
uses the same 11/03 as a console device that the 11/780 does.


>	I am not sure what was buried in the VAX-11/750 (other 
>than a second TU-58)

An Intel 8085. What second TU-58? There is *a* TU-58 in the 11/750.


>		     - but try removing the PRO-380 from the
>front of a VAX-8000 series machine and see how it boots. (The
>VAX-8000 machines are the last of the VAX-11 series that had the
>PDP-11 instruction set). The PRO-380 of course has the KDJ11 chip
>for performance similar to the PDP-11/70 - hence the power of the
>VAX-8000 series.

Again, this is noise. Any VAX (in the classes we are talking about)
depends on the console processor only to start/halt, to load
microcode, to load main memory at boot time, and other such mundane
tasks. The VAX does not "use" the PDP in any way that would affect
the VAX's runtime "power".


Someone's been telling you tall tales, John. 

-- 
Bob Krzaczek                                               boba@ccird1.cci.com
