In <1991Sep6.140717.1@venus.ycc.yale.edu>, Tang Wong writes:

>My friend is trying to set up a print queue on a VAXstation 2000 which 
>is also part of a cluster.  The first question is what is the name of 
>the printer port.  The system manager who is a fellow student does not
>know the answer.  He has tried all of the device names in SHOW DEVICE 
>but none of them worked.

Strange. We have thrown out our VS2000's a while ago, but I think the
printer port was CSA0 or CSA1. If you are still running UIS, you can try
to $ SHOW LOGICAL UIS$PRINT_DESTINATION. That defaults to the printer 
port on the machine unless you have redirected the screen-dumps elsewhere.


>                          The second question is how to prevent users 
>in the cluster from using his printer; ie, how to set protection for 
>the queue.

If, for argument's sake, you queue is called VS2000$PRINT and the user who
needs access is called TANG then you do the following:

$ MCR AUTHORIZE
UAF> ADD/IDENTIFIER VS2000PRINT
UAF> GRANT/IDENTIFIER VS2000PRINT TANG
UAF> EXIT

This creates the identifier "VS2000PRINT" and grants it to the user TANG.
Next you type:

$ SET QUEUE/PROTECTION=WORLD VS2000$PRINT

That will stop the "world" from accessing the queue (normally the "world"
can Write). Finally you type:

$ SET ACL/OBJ=QUEUE/ACL=(IDENTIFIER=VS2000PRINT,ACCESS=WRITE) VS2000$PRINT
                                             
This will set an AccessControlList on VS2000$PRINT which allows holders of
the "VS2000PRINT" Identifier to Write to the queue VS2000$PRINT. [It may be
a good idea to grant that id to SYSTEM as well ?!]

Note: You need a lot of privileges to do all this.

Cheers

Bernhard Fabricius
