>No VAX console processor plays any part in the execution of VAX
>instructions. (Pedantic exeptions include the interruption of
>instruction execution by typing Ctrl-P on a console, and using
>console commands to speed up the VAX CPU clock).   In any case,
>750s, 730s, and Microvaxes are all implemented without any separate
>console processor.  (Pendants may mention the the 8085 in the KC750 RDM).

Sorry, the 11/730 has an Intel 8085 buried on its WCS board that has
the same responsibility as the 11/780's PDP 11/03.  It reads microcode
files off of one of the TU58s (file by file, interesting to watch)
into the WCS and handles the console commands at the >>> prompt.  The
8085 even boots itself from a special boot-block on the console TU58
with the VAX console program before it does anything.  How do I know
all this?  I own one of the bloody things, sits in my basement.  It is
a personal playtoy and of course I do not have DEC maintenance so I
keep it going myself.  I ran one where I work for a while before-hand.
The console processor for this SPU is documented in the VAX Hardware
Handbook (C) 1982 (for 11/730s, 11/750s, and 11/780s & 782s).  The VAX
11/750 (when ides doesn't have an RDI board) uses actual SPU microcode
to do the console processing and microcode is indeed in ROM.

>These machines have boot ROMs with VAX instructions that load a
>bootblock from a system disk, and then execute the bootblock.
> 
