(Bob Krzaczek) writes, showing that he too can be guilty of producing "noise":

> No, the console PDP of an 11/78x series VAX is T-11 based.

That would have been quite a trick, since the T-11 (billed as "a PDP-11/23 on a
chip") did not come along until the early 80's.  

The VAX-11/780 appeared in 1978 (hence the model number), and was of course in
design and pre-production for many years before that. 

The console processor of a 780 is an LSI-11.  (No, not an LSI-11/02.  That was
the version with a dual-height CPU card.  The LSI-11 had a quad-height CPU
card.)  And it most emphatically did *not* use a single-chip CPU, which is what 
the T-11 is. 

