Has anyone with ultrix 4.2 had success backing up on exabyte tapes?
I am able to tar/cpio small chunks, say 2-8 Mb or so. Any thing
bigger and it just freezes the system and I have to reset the system
without syncing, really dangerous. Three times it rebooted clean
but the fourth time boot failed.  I had to manually fsck /usr
partition and basically I lost /lib/tmac/*, /usr/include/idl/c/*
and /lib/learn/editor*  files.  The last two set are no big deal,
but I had to copy tmac files from another system to get man pages
working.  Now I am really scared.

The problem I think is that my machine is not able to recognize
that this tape can hold LOTS of data, even if I explicitly say so.

For example, if I say "tar cvVbf 32 /dev/rmt0h /"
it backs up some files and then the system load shoots up to approx 8-9
and everything freezes. I can't telnet even from any other machine
and the only recourse is to reset the machine.

After rebooting, I can use "mt -f /dev/rmt0h rewind"  or retension etc.
Looking at "tar tvVbf 32 /dev/rmt0h ", I get a short listing and then
a message that rest of the archive is on another tape.

Even when I tried "tartvVsbf 2000000 32 /dev/rmt0h /", where s and 2000000
specify that archive can be 2 million blocks of 512 bytes, I
get into the same problem.

Exactly the same problem with dump. In the beginning dump says,
to backup / it will need 1250 (or so) blocks of 10240 bytes
on 1.00 disks. It goes thru pass I,II (maps), III (dumping dirs)
and freezes at IV(dumping files).  Again I tried specifying the tape
size via "dump 0unBsf 8 1000000 /dev/rmt0h /" but no luck.

Besides my / is less than 15Mb, so even if dump thinks I have minimum
length tape, that should be enough.

To make everything more difficult, anDATAco supplies a 10 page
manual titled "users guide exabyte tape subsystems for SUN WORKSTATIONS";
I phoned them but I was told they have NO OTHER MANUAL!

(By the way, their example uses st1 whereas on dec I had to use tz1).

So far I have tried to get help from the following sources without
much success.

a). Tim Torg, "backup expert" at stanford sweet hall: Tim says he has used
    exabyte only on suns; asked me to call the distributor.

b). anDATAco in san diego: the "tech support" said that they have no
    manual for a dec machine; that they have started selling exabyte
    to dec customers only recently; that he "hasn't got a clue" as to
    what could be wrong. (his words)

c). exabyte in colorado: these folks were closed; I'll try on Friday.

d). Dec Atlanta support: Mr Arthur ??: "I don't know if we support exabyte
    tape; I'll log a call for you and the field support will call you"

e). dec field support (UNOFFICIAL response): talk to "dec fellow" at
    stanford, who is, in a  way, an assistant for Tim(a), and will
    go to Tim for help.  Well folks, that completes the circle.

What can I say?  Where would people like me go for help,
if not for usenet?

Thanks for listening. --raj

P.S. Go ahead flame me for so much cross-posting, but provide
     some help too. :-)
