In article <1991Sep13.175023.2026@cci632.cci.com>, boba@cci632.cci.com (Bob Krzaczek) writes:
> At the risk of moving this away from noise and into actual information, I
> thought the "11/23 on a chip" was the F-11? Or, are the T-11 and F-11
> more closely related than I previously thought? Given my goof on the LSI-11
> and T-11...

  The 11/23 _is_ the F-11. I don't know if you could say "on a chip", since
the MMU is on another chip, the [optional] FPP on a third, and the [optional]
CIS on either 1 additional (production units) or 3 additional (prototypes)
chips. For embedded controller-type applications, the F-11 is sometimes used
without the MMU, yielding a single-chip implementation. There is also an op-
tional "hot" floating point unit, the FPF11 (not to be confused with the FP11-
F), which is a whole board full of AMD2901 bit-slice processors. It just draws
power from the backplane (either Unibus or Q-bus - it's jumperable) and con-
nects to the CPU board via a 40-pin plug into the appropriate chip socket.

  The T-11 (also known as the 11/21 when on some of DEC's boards) is a slower
chip, which is also missing some of the instructions us modern PDP-11-er's
think of as the "core" set, although they are still technically options. It
is used on DEC boards such as the DEUNA and the RQDX1/2/3, as well as on ap-
plication coprocessors such as the KXT11's. The latter usage has been pretty
much supplanted by the J-11-based coprocessors.

  The LSI-11 chipset (original LSI-11) was developed by Western Digital. It
is a generic microcoded 16-bit processor in a 4-chip (as I recall) set. There
are 3 known microcode sets for it: DEC PDP-11 (obvious), WD-16 (the vendor's
instruction set, with identical PDP-11 assembler mnemonics but different binary
values for opcodes), and Pascal MicroEngine, which implemented the UCSD P-code
machine in hardware. Unfortunately, just as that implementation was completed,
Softech changed the P-machine description and new code would no longer run on
the MicroEngine. There was an optional socket for additional microcode on the
DEC boards. DIBOL would used an optional DIS (DIBOL Instruction Set) chip if
you had it. There was also a custom chip for the military, and one for Western
Electric (some early digital circuit controllers had embedded LSI-11's).

  The J-11 (which DEC calls the "11/70 on a chip") is the first true "full"
implementation of the PDP-11 on a single chip. All processor options except
CIS are implemented on the chip (CIS was withdrawn by this point. I am told
that the unused mounting pads on the underside of the J-11 were for CIS by
some people, while others maintain it was for user microcode, a la the PDP-
11/60.) A faster "hot" floating point is available in the companion FPJ11
chip. Note that the F-11 needed an additional chip to do _any_ FP, while the
J-11 can do slower FP on its own.

