Let us get some things stright here....
The LSI processors known to man (that is common ones) are these:
LSI11 and LSI11/2 (a quad board and the one you find in the 78X and a dual
board). Here the PDP is in four chips and a fifth one is the FP and CIS.
Micro/T-11 is a single chip microprocessor. It has these features:
16 bit microproc in 40 pin encapsulation. Selectable 8-BIT or 16-BIT bus.
Dynamic RAM refresh capability and PDP-11 basic instruction set and adressing
modes. This processor was mainly used for stand alone ROM-based controlling
systems. You will NOT find it in a 78X's console.
The F-set or the 11/23 has extended LSI11 (22 bit adress range). It can
run in 16/18 or 22 bit mode. It can run almost all kinds of PDP software and
as floating point and CIS as options (CIS is Commersial Instruction Set wich is
a kind of string handling instructions in assembler to enable COBOL (*LEPROSY!*
(make the sign of a cross with your fingers!)) preformance boost in your PDP).
A huge 6-chip capsule with almost double width compared with ordinary
capsules. The F-based computers were a commersial product at the time and 
was NOT used as console on the 78X series. DEC seems to use 'surplus' on
their consoles. :) Oh yes. No seperate I and D space on these processors.
The J-set is the PDP11/70 on a chip (mild exhageration). A huge spider with
two chip on the white ceramic carrier. It has it all (save for CIS) but there
is a floating point hardware turbo chip aviable (it is originally emulated in
micro code). The J-based boards have cache and separate I and D space. Still
used today. Good processor. Likable and pretty swift.
The difference between the 780 and the 785 is that the LSI11 based RT11
computer has more memory in the 785.
Let me see here *mumble, search search search* Ah.
Quote: An LSI-11 micro processor KD11-F including a 4 K by 16 bit semi
conductor radnom access memory (RAM). VAX HARDWARE HANDBOOK 1982-83 about the
780. Unquote.
I do not recall the numbers on the 785 (we junked ours a while back) but
i think it is in the region of 32 kilo.
Funny.... It is like yesterday when the huge 785 arrived. More computing power
than you ever needed. It left the pen in a couple of containers. Dead, fossile-
like and dinosaurous. A final thought about the 780 series.
The 780 is the MIPS machine. It makes about 1 MIPS. Thirteen years later DEC
launched the DEC-Mpp 1200. It makes (optimistically measured) 26000 MIPS and
has 16000 plus processors in the biggie one. Yes, I hate MIPS too, but still...
It is what the sales droids say and said. :)
Let us talk more 7XX machines. I just love them. :)
