In article <1991Sep13.145730.3878@maspar.ch>, root@maspar.ch (Bruno Pape) writes:
   > Hello,
   > 
   > I have about 20 or 30 Ultrix distribution tapes that I would like
   > to use for scratch tapes, but it seems that there is some additional
   > write protection on these tapes, in addition to the little flip
   > latch, is this true?  Is there any way to use these tapes or are
   > they bound for the waste basket?

I doubt there is any other write protection, but have you got a TK50
or TK70 drive?  A TK70 can read TK50 tapes, but cannot write to them.
All distribution is done on TK50, that is what your tapes will be, if 
your drive is a TK70 it will refuse to write to them.

I'm told that you can bulk-erase TK50 tapes to remove their formatting and
restore them to virgin tape, a TK70 will then format them for itself
and allow you to use them, *BUT* DEC say that the real cartridges for a TK70
(TK52s) have higher quality tape. Using bulk-erased TK50s may not be reliable.
Probably OK for scratch use/transfer but not for backups.
