In article <1991Sep15.133339.23073@usenet.ins.cwru.edu>, an288@cleveland.Freenet.Edu (Mark Hittinger) writes:
> Now how much do you want to bet that the true driving force behind the high
> end pdp-11 development is the need for a faster HSC-70?

  I strongly doubt it, for a number of reasons:

  1) The CPU chip on the '93 is the same speed as that on an '83. All the en-
     hancements were related to the memory and bus interface on the board.

  2) The HSC's don't use the PDP-11 processor cards - just the CPU chip on an
     extended hex module.

  3) The CPU in the HSC doesn't move any data - it just directs the various
     parts of the HSC to copy data. The new HSC's (60/90) still use J-11's,
     but get their performace boost from data caching.

  4) The 11/93 board was done outside of DEC. If the project was "strategic"
     to DEC, they'd have done it in-house.

> On the subject of old DEC gear as hobby equipment - just say no.  You'll
> wind up with a lot less headache if you just get yourself a 386 box.  Nostalgia
> is great but there are legal issues about software licenses which should
> preclude honest hobby use (hahaha).  How about us TOPS-10 guys who would like
> nothing better than some kind of 10 in the basement?  We certainly can't get
> any software licenses and its been dead for years!

  Tell that to the members of the DECUS South Pacific Chapter, where PDP-11 OS
and layered products are available to hobbyists from DEC, legally, for the
princely sum of $25 per. Now ask why that isn't the case here in the US.

  However, note that if you buy a used PDP-11 that has an OS license, you can
transfer it. Call your local DEC office and ask for a license transfer form.
Make note of the name of the person you spoke to and the date of the call.
Then, if you don't get the form, or if you do get it and send it back and no-
thing happens, is that your fault? Normally, they will send it back in a few
months and you'll own a nice OS license for your hobby system.

