Folklore has it that when our 11/780 was installed (must have been
late 1978 or early 1979, long before my time) it fell off the truck on
the way from the airport (the climate here is pretty benign, so they
were moving it on a flatbed with just a tarp over it). The damage
to the frame was still visible (the machine was retired a year ago);
the FSE pointed out to me hat the frame is very bend and obviously was
straightened by brute force (big f***ing hammer), and one corner still
looks very mangled.  Supposedly that 780 was the first one ever to be
installed outside the continental US (it clearly was the first one in
the state, and probably only Alaska could have competed in early
deliveries).  At that time DEC Hawaii had no field service people who
knew how to set up a VAX, so one or two guys from software support had
to do the cabling themselves.

Contrast that with a 3100, which we broke by just driving it across
the parking lot on a lab cart (not just connector rattled loose, the
system board had to be replaced). They don't make them like the used
to :-) On the other hand, the 3100s can ride through a short power
glitch (unfortunately frequent in the land of Hawaiian Electric) which
would reliably crash the 780 :-)

Back to our regularly scheduled program ...

