Software you refer is called EWS. What you need is a VMS or ULTRIX load host
and (minimum) 6MB memory in VS2000. Performance (graphics) of EWS is much 
better than performance of VS2000 with VMS or ULTRIX. After installing EWS 
there is no use for disks anymore (at least currently), but before removing
them, you should check that your VS2000's are revision high enoug, because
older VS2000's stop functioning if you remove internal disk due to change
in voltage levels in CPU board. Generally, EWS makes your VS2000 acceptable
as terms of performance again, but it doesn't make it 'fly like rocket' ;-)
 

From:	IN%"mostler@omega.sim.es.com"  3-SEP-1991 17:03:25.56

We are currently running both VS2000 and VS3100 using Dec's windowing software.
The machines are using DecStation 5000's as boothosts.  Some of the
requirments that we have seen to get performance from these machines are;

The Boot host needs 12-16 meg additional of memory for each
additional terminal that you add. 

The Swap space on your boot host needs to be big enough for
its own applications, plus enough for each application that the client
will be running. For example we are running interleaf publishing software on 
these " X-terminals" Interleaf recommends 40 megabytes for each application, 
so the bootserver is running 100 megabytes for itself plus and additional 
40 for the x terminal that is running interleaf.  The other application that 
we run is a cad program that is graphic intensive.  It is not nearly as fast 
on the VS2000 under Dec's product, but it still is adequate.  The VS2000 must 
have 14 megabytes of memory or it doesn't perform well at all.


