I shall continue to repair my equioppment down to componet level, 
because for me that is the cheapest and quickest thing to do. Suppose 
tha your 386 won't boot. Presumably you swap motherboard, PSU, disk
controller etc. until it does. I do the following:
Check PSU with voltmeter - say that's OK.
check/change disk control registers from the panel - assume I find an
error here.
Put controller cards on extender, and find dud chip with logic probe/scope
Total time to diagnose about 1 hour - not much more than board swapping an
386 machine. Then I get the replacement chip from my spares box, and put
it in - No sending off for expensive boards that I may not need, and no
dismantling other people's machines to get parts to try in mine. You don't
need much test-gear either  - I fixed my RK11-C using simply a battery
testing voltmeter - it was a logic fault, in the checksum adder board - a
7474 flip-flop had failed. total cost of repair - 35p. 

Another advantage of the PDP11 is that being made of TTL chips (the early
UNIBUS ones anyway), it is possible to learn an awful lot about how
computers work and the internal processes from them. I learnt more about
floating point arithmetic from the FP11-B manual than from any book on
computer science.

I do have a PC compatable (Actually a genuine IBM XT), but prefer the
PDP11 design, construction and complete philosophy. It's a godd design,
the IBM is not. 

  Tony 'PDP11 Hacker' Duell


