sspatter@uokmax.ecn.uoknor.edu (Samuel S Patterson) writes:
|>Hi.
|>
|>      Okay, rather simple question.  When I hit control halt/sys on
|>my PDP The SR DISP light goes out, the buss error light comes on, and
|>the address data panel stays on.  I did the system tests the book
|>sug-
|>gests.  IE, pulling out the M9302 card at the end of the unibus. 
|>Anyways,
|>The results of the tests tell me that I have a BUS Grant problem. 
|>What
|>I need to know is how to fix that.  Where is the bus grant generated
|>and
|>what would cause it to malfunction?  I have all the cards for 2
|>complete
|>PDP-1134A systems so it is mostly a matter of finding out which cards
|>are
|>good and which aren't and the same with the backplanes.  I should
|>have
|>enough parts to get one PDP running if I'm lucky.  Thanks in Advance.

It's been a while since I've worked on a PDP-11/34a, but I'll give it a
shot anyway. When an interrupt is requested, the processor acknowleges
it by telling the interrupting device to place its interrupt vector on
the bus. This is done by asserting a signal called BUS GRANT. This
signal starts at the processor and works its way down the bus, through
each card, until the interrupting device sees it and swallows it; if two
devices requested the same interrupt at the same time, the one
*electrically closer* to the processor swallows the BUS GRANT first and
the other devices don't see it.

Since the BUS GRANT line must go into each board and out again, an empty
slot poses a little problem. To fix this, you stick a little card called
a Grant Card into all the empty slots. This card has a foil jumper
between the Bus Grant In and Bus Grant Out pins, so that the Bus Grant
signal gets passed on to the next slot.

The most common reason for a problem like you describe is empty slots. I
no longer remember which connector in the slot the grant card gets stuck
into, so someone else will have to tell you. The grant cards themselves
are a dime a dozen, since the average BA-11K box came stuffed with them.
I still have two or three in my desk drawer, and I haven't worked on a
Unibus machine in years.

--
