    I'm sorry that I missed Steve's original posting (one of the hazards of
    being on the road so much). And, I'm sorry that the solution wasn't as
    easy as a single phone call or looking at a reference manual (which is
    the way I would like it to be). 

    There is some information I'd like to share though:

	* Ultrix has supported the definition of 3rd party SCSI devices since
	  v4.0 (although the documentation for implementing needs examples).

	* All of the DECstation hardware is SCSI 2 based (both the SII chip
	  in the DS2100/3100 and the NCR53C94 chip in the DS5000/XXX). 

	* With the release of Ultrix v4.2 the Ultrix SCSI driver is a full
	  SCSI 2 implementation.

	* While a SCSI 2 host (system) can communicate with a SCSI 1 target 
	  (read disk,tape or CD-ROM); there is no guarantee that a SCSI 1
	  target will cope.

		Example: The SCSI 1 specification did not set a response
			 time for a target to respond to a SCSI buss reset.
			 Therefore, some SCSI 1 devices will not respond
			 within the 250ms timeframe recommended by the SCSI 2
			 specification (the DS5000/200 waits ~480ms).

	* The ANSI SCSI specifications are a hardware protocol. The CAM effort
	  (Common Access Method) is an industry sponsored protocol (and a 
	  proposal to the IEEE) for a common SCSI software interface. The
	  Ultrix SCSI driver is being re-written to a CAM compliant format
	  and should be shipping early in calendar '92 (it's in Field Test
	  currently). With the CAM interface it becomes easy to define a 
	  whole series of ANSI compliant SCSI devices (including scanners,
	  printers, etc.).

	* A new version of the SCSI PROM for the DS5000/200 and the PMAZ-AA
	  SCSI host adapter has been generated. I will attempt to get the
	  image files put up on gatekeeper for public use (so that those 
	  brave souls who wish to blast their own PROMs may do so) and I'll
	  certainly post a notice with the ECO/FCO number as soon as I can
	  get ahold of it. This new PROM is about as forgiving of third
	  party devices as we can make it.

	* At the risk of being redundant to my posting of a couple weeks back;
	  Digital's TRI/ADD Program (which is where I work and is a part of
	  the workstations engineering group) is more than happy to work with
	  any SCSI System or Option vendor to help make their products run on
	  any of the DECstation/DECsystem products. We have helped a number of
	  Disk and Tape vendors to get their hardware running on our platforms
	  and will continue to do so. If Fujitsu is interested they have only
	  to contact us and they'll get the same assistance that we've given
	  to the others.

		NOTE: We are not a customer hot-line. Our focus is Third Party
		      vendors for SCSI, VME, TURBOchannel, Futurebus+ and now
		      Access.bus; so we would prefer that you have your option
		      vendors contact us. With over 270 member institutions we
		      stay pretty busy as is. I've included the contact info
		      at the bottom of this posting.

	* A subset of products that we believe work on our platforms can be
	  found in the TRI/ADD Shippable Products Catalog.

		gatekeeper	pub/DEC/TriAdd/TAcatalog.ps

	  No warranty of the vendor product information is made, nor is it a
	  complete listing (I haven't figured out a reasonable representation
	  for all of the disk and tape models).


