In 1975/76 my friend Graham Klyne wrote a PDP-8 simulator running on a PDP11/10
under RT-11. Great fun. If you ran FOCAL (remember FOCAL?) on a PDP-8, it would
tell you what particular model it was running on. Naturally, we wondered what
it would make of the simulator. On running it, it went something like ...

    FOCAL (version) running on PDP [loooong pause] 8.

Presumably the pause was the interval when FOCAL grubbed around in lots of
different instruction sequences looking for behaviour expected from the
higher-end models of the '8, made visible by the treacle of interpretation.

[We also had a wire joining the *real* PDP-8 to the PDP-11, allowing us to run
OS-8 from a pseudo-disc on the 11's RK05. When we first tried this out, it was
discovered that the engineer had wired the connection up back-to-front,
reversing the bit-order on transfer. Do I correctly recall the PDP-8 bit 
numbering being backwards, ie, bit 0 was the *most* significant?]

Oh happy days.
--

