In article <1991Sep2.074450.4928@cs.aukuni.ac.nz>, rob@cs.aukuni.ac.nz (Rob Burrowes ) writes:
> Can someone tell me where I can find a detailed description of the booting of
> a VAX. The hardware manuals outline the process but I want to know the finer
> details.
> 
> Which VAX you ask? Well I'll settle for any or all.
> 
	Well, lets start at the beginning - the VAX 11/780 was 
based on the PDP-11 instruction set with 32-bit data. It used a 
PDP-11/23 buried down in the bottom to get it going. Some say 
that the PDP-11 was necessary to load MICROcode every time the
VAX was restarted - some (PDP-11 SIG types) say that the upper 
deck processing was all `show and tell' and that all the real 
crunching was done by the 11/23 chip. This, of course, explains 
why the VAX-11/782 (or was it the VAX-11/785) had to have the 
floating point accelerator chip added to the PDP-11/23 board to 
achieve the enhanced performance on the later models.

	I am not sure what was buried in the VAX-11/750 (other 
than a second TU-58) - but try removing the PRO-380 from the 
front of a VAX-8000 series machine and see how it boots. (The
VAX-8000 machines are the last of the VAX-11 series that had the 
PDP-11 instruction set). The PRO-380 of course has the KDJ11 chip 
for performance similar to the PDP-11/70 - hence the power of the 
VAX-8000 series.

	SERIOUSLY - the boot code for each machine will probably 
be different - you should be able to get listings from the boot 
roms if you cant get any help from this newsgroup. 
Regards, John G.
