In article <192@w2xo.pgh.pa.us>, durham@w2xo.pgh.pa.us (Jim Durham) writes:
> I have a Rev. 1 version of the TK-50 controller board (M7546).
> What does it take to upgrade this module to Rev. 6? This is a
> personal Microvax and I can't afford the usual channels. If it's
> just a hardware change, then I can do that, but if it's a prom
> change, then is it possible to purchase the e-proms?

  I assume you mean the revision number set on the DIP switch, which is there
solely to confuse the operating system 8-}. Having said that, the current
revision number is 9.

  The actual revision information is found by looking on the back of the mod-
ule handle, where it is either punched in, stamped on, or stuck on with Brady
(tm) markers.

  There are 3 changes to get from where you are to where you want to be:

  1) Replace E3 with 065L1 FPLS chip
  2) Replace firmware with 330E5/331E5 ROMs
  3) Various etch cuts/jumps

  The hard part is #1. This is a cusyom programmed logic sequencer chip. I'd
suggest you go for a flat-rate exchange with either DEC or a 3rd-party pro-
vider (like Efficient Field Service). Be sure to specify that you want the
board upgraded to the current revision level and marked appropriately when
you send it back, if you use a 3rd-party service outfit.

  You _should_ be able to order the parts from DEC's Unlisted Parts Service
at (603) 884-5000 if you're really desperate for an inexpensive solution.

