>	Well, lets start at the beginning - the VAX 11/780 was 
>based on the PDP-11 instruction set with 32-bit data. It used a 
>PDP-11/23 buried down in the bottom to get it going.

This is just all wrong.  The article by Streker (I think) in Bell,
Sieworiek and Newell explains why DEC chose the VAX architecture,
which is not a PDP-11 with 32-bit data, over one of the competing
alternatives, which was a real PDP-11 with 32-bit data.

The console processor in an 11/780 was an 11/03, not an 11/23.  All
the LSI-11 did was load microcode into the KA780 from an RX01 console
floppy, interpret console mode commands, and execute the command
file(s) on the console floopy that loaded the initial boostrap program
(VMB on VMS and Ultrix systems, something else for Unix) into main
memory, and start it executing.


No VAX console processor plays any part in the execution of VAX
instructions. (Pedantic exeptions include the interruption of
instruction execution by typing Ctrl-P on a console, and using
console commands to speed up the VAX CPU clock).   In any case,
750s, 730s, and Microvaxes are all implemented without any separate
console processor.  (Pendants may mention the the 8085 in the KC750 RDM).
These machines have boot ROMs with VAX instructions that load a
bootblock from a system disk, and then execute the bootblock.
 
The rest of the posting was equally incorrect.  If anyone wants, I can
post the real data, with reference, but I would prefer to e-mail
complete and accurate answers to Rob Burrowes, who started this
thread.


I'm posting rather than replying to forestall the possibility of
anyone being misled by John Geremin's posting.  Are these machines so
ancient that the net's collective memory has forgotten?  Or is this
just misinformation from a single PDP-11 enthusiast in Australia?

--Jonathan Stone


