I think the VMS UIC is pretty much the same as a Unix uid/gid. Originally two
8-bit numbers, one for group id and one for user within the group. It also
existed in the RSX11 and RSTS operating systems for PDPs, which is certainly
where VMS got it from & quite possibly where Unix did (first Unix was a
PDP O/S)

VMS uses it mostly for file protection, most user access and accounting is
done by Username, which need not have any particular relation to UIC. SUN's
DECnet product (DNI) supplies a uid for connection requests from Unix to VMS,
where VMS normally expects a username. A certain amount of uid/username mapping
is done there, perhaps thats what the TCP/IP product does?

One recent change is that from VMS 5, the UIC isn't limited to 2 8-bit numbers,
but can now be (I think) 2 16-bit numbers, so there may not be a direct mapping
between UIC and uid/gid any more.

