In article <1991Sep9.192716.14199@decus.com.au> geremin@decus.com.au writes:
>	Well, lets start at the beginning - the VAX 11/780 was 
>based on the PDP-11 instruction set with 32-bit data.

No, the VAX is a completely different processor than the PDP. The
architecture is *not* similar. The PDP uses word opcodes that
included addressing modes. The VAX uses byte opcodes with trailing
arguments. Some VAXen included a PDP-11 *emulation* mode.


>                                                      It used a 
>PDP-11/23 buried down in the bottom to get it going.

No, the console PDP was T-11 based, I think (it was either LSI or
LSI/2, I can't recall which right now).


>                                                     Some say 
>that the PDP-11 was necessary to load MICROcode every time the
>VAX was restarted 

Some people are wrong, then. The PDP would load the microcode into
the VAX, but you only had to do this once per machine reset. You
could halt/continue/start the VAX to your heart's content without
reloading the microcode.


>                   - some (PDP-11 SIG types) say that the upper 
