Random thoughts about PDP-8 machines:

Machines related to the PDP-8 included the PDP-5, the PDP-12 and the
LINC-8.  These used a fixed-length 12-bit word (two 6-bit "bytes").
The direct memory addressing could handle only 4096 words (yes, 4K),
but bank-switching could let the machine handle up to 32K words.
The PDP-8 on which I first learned programming was equipped with a
fixed-head disk drive, the capacity of which was 256K words.  Tape
was typically a DECtape--a unique form of magnetic media that was
eventually shoved aside by the floppy disk.

To start up the early processors, you had to toggle in a bootstrap
program (the RIM loader) from switches on the front panel.  The normal
user interface was a Teletype Model 33, with paper-tape reader/punch.

System software available at various times included:

  (single-user)

  4K Disk Monitor
  PS/8
  OS/8
  COS-300
  OS/278 (I think, in later versions)

  (multiple-user !)

  TSS/8 (also sold under the name Edusystem-50)

Programming languages tended to be truncated in capability from versions
on larger systems.  There were interpreters or compilers for

  ALGOL 60 subset
  BASIC
  DIBOL  (DEC's business-oriented language)
  FOCAL  (DEC's original plastic  language)

Not to forget, of course, the good old PAL-8 assembler.

A full FORTRAN 66 compiler was available under OS/8 (although you had
to have the hardware FPP-12 floating point processor, one full rack of
wire-wrap cards, to use double precision).  Alas, the double-precision
trigonometric functions in this compiler's runtime library used the
wrong value for pi, but compiler listings did greet you with special
sentiments on Christmas Day and Independence Day.

That PDP-8 was put out to pasture in 1978, replaced by the newer, more
capable PDP-11/34, running that exotic operating system, RSTS/E!

