A static prompt can be assigned using the command

    $ SET PROMPT="''F$ENVIRONMENT("DEFAULT")'> "

A _dynamic_ prompt string requires a recursive procedure such as:

    $! PROMPT.COM
    $  X = 'F$VERIFY(0)'
    $PROMPT:
    $  READ/PROMPT="''F$ENVIRONMENT("DEFAULT")'> " SYS$COMMAND P1
    $  DEFINE/NOLOG SYS$INPUT SYS$COMMAND
    $  'P1'
    $  GOTO PROMPT
    $  EXIT

In fact, you can put _anything_ in the command string. I wrote this 
procedure years ago for a friend who used to work on a MULTICS system
and like having the current date and time returned as a prompt. The
same effect can be achieved in the above procedure by replacing
    $ READ/PROMPT="''F$ENVIRONMENT("DEFAULT")'> " SYS$COMMAND P1
with
    $ READ/PROMPT="''F$TIME()'> " SYS$COMMAND P1

Having said that, however, I wouldn't advise using the procedure (just
enjoy it!) as you're effectively working within a sub-environment and any
error will abort the procedure (as will typing `EXIT'). 

